# core/orphans - Which elements take part in no relationship at all?
#
# Answers: elements that are neither source nor target of any qualified
# relationship, one row each.
#
# Does NOT prove: that an element is unused or safe to delete. Common innocent
# causes: it was drawn on a diagram as context and never connected; its
# relationships live in a model that was not loaded; or it is a routing construct
# the notation permits to stand alone.
#
# Useful as a model-quality reading rather than a hit list. A high orphan count
# concentrated in one model usually means a partial export, which core/models and
# core/inventory will confirm faster than inspecting the elements.
#
# THE ABSENCE TEST SPANS EVERY SEMANTIC GRAPH, through the independent scopes
# `semantic2` and `semantic3`, while the element itself is still found in one. That
# distinction is the whole point. These tests used to sit inside the element's own
# scope, which asks "is there a relationship in the same graph the element was typed
# in" - and since the 1.3 layout the semantic graph is partitioned per input file, so
# that question is bounded by which file the converter happened to read. A
# relationship in a sibling partition would leave its endpoint reported as an orphan,
# and an orphan list is read as a list of things nobody is using.
#
# No such case appears in the committed fixtures, and none was found in a large
# aggregate export either: converters keep a relationship with its endpoints. So this
# is a correctness fix under partitioning rather than a measured behaviour change, and
# it costs nothing - the scope widens from one semantic graph to all of them, not to
# the whole dataset, because a qualified relationship is a semantic fact and no views
# or provenance graph holds one.
#
# Types are concatenated rather than given a row each, as in core/view-contents.
# Elements are multi-typed - a core class plus a notation class - so one row per type
# reads as several elements: measured on a large export, 1,530 rows described 1,009
# elements. A count that inflates by whatever the optional columns multiply is worse
# than useless in a quality reading, because the inflation looks like severity.
#
# STR() around ?type is load-bearing, not tidying. GROUP_CONCAT over IRIs is a type
# error, and an erroring aggregate yields an unbound result rather than a failure - so
# without it every row arrives with an empty types column while the count still looks
# right.
#
# Parameters: LIMIT
{{PREFIXES}}
SELECT ?element ?label (GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT STR(?type); separator=", ") AS ?types) ?model
WHERE {
  {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic}}
    ?element a {{ROLE:element_class}} .
    OPTIONAL { ?element {{PATH:label}} ?label }
    OPTIONAL {
      ?element a ?type .
      FILTER(?type != {{ROLE:element_class}} && ?type != {{ROLE:concept_class}})
    }
    OPTIONAL { ?element {{ROLE:part_of}} ?model }
  {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic2}}
      ?rel a {{ROLE:relationship_class}} ; {{ROLE:rel_source}} ?element .
    {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  }
  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic3}}
      ?rel a {{ROLE:relationship_class}} ; {{ROLE:rel_target}} ?element .
    {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  }
}
GROUP BY ?element ?label ?model
ORDER BY ?model ?label ?element
LIMIT {{LIMIT}}
